      "content": "It's not just about how we've traditionally studied it, but also about the underlying biology and genetics. Melanoma, for instance, is more common in darker-skinned individuals due to the increased production of melanin, which acts as a natural sunscreen. However, this increased melanin production also makes it harder for the immune system to detect cancerous cells. Additionally, certain genetic variants, such as those found in the CDKN2A gene, are more prevalent in African Americans and are associated with an increased risk of melanoma. These genetic differences can influence the presentation and progression of the disease.",
